    desc: 'The Commodore Amiga defined a creative-computing visual culture: Deluxe Paint pixel illustration, HAM-mode 4096-color showpieces, Boing Ball demo physics, and Workbench\'s grey-and-blue desktop look. TV stations (like BBC\'s 1990s graphics departments) used Amigas for broadcast art; demoscene groups (Kefrens, Red Sector) pushed copper bars and dot pools; and tracked MOD music gave the machine a sound identity. The Amiga look — chunky fonts, saturated pixel gradients, cheerful gadget chrome — persists in retro-computing revivals, PiStorm-era hardware and A-EON continuations, and Deluxe Paint-era brushes seeded today\'s pixel-art tooling.',
