    sum: 'The picture you stared at for three minutes: borders, logos and hope.',
    desc: 'On the Commodore 64, the loading screen was half the product: while cassette tape hissed through loader bars, players stared at title pictures squeezed into the C64\'s attribute-grid — multicolor char-cell art with forced colors per 8x8 cell — produced by graphics houses like The Designers Republic precursors, Bob Wakelin and Oliver Frey for Zzap!64-adjacent studios. Ocean, Imagine and Hewson commissioned painted-to-pixel conversions that were often better than the games; cracktros later gave the same slots to demos. The grid-constrained look (two colors per cell, charset glyphs as texture) is a distinctive pixel-art dialect still imitated in modern indie covers.',
