    sum: 'Fin-headed rocketships and zap-guns — the future drawn in bright pulp ink.',
    desc: 'Raygun Gothic is the term (coined by William Gibson in "The Gernsback Continuum") for the pulp-SF visual language of 1926-1950s: swooping finned rocketships, bubble canopies, needle-nosed zap guns, dials-and-bubbles control panels and underdome cities — the future as Amazing Stories cover art. Its designers borrowed from streamliners, deco and world\'s-fair futurism; its legacy runs through Forbidden Planet props, Tomorrowland, The Iron Giant, and every toy rocket that means "space age". The style is optimistic, drawn-curve smooth, and permanently associated with chrome and starlight.',
