    desc: 'Clockpunk imagines the technological future built on Renaissance engineering: da Vinci-style flying machines, self-moving automata, cathedral-sized orreries, spring-driven arms and gear-punk weapons, all rendered in woodcut, sepia paper and polished brass. The term — a branch of the -punk family coined online in the 2000s — draws on Leonardo\'s notebooks, the mechanical curiosity cabinets of Rudolf II, and 16th-century automaton masterpieces. Games (Thief\'s Hammerite machines, Assassin\'s gear cities), novels and cosplayers use the aesthetic for a future that smells of wood shavings, hot metal and church incense instead of oil.',
