    sum: 'Triangles everywhere: shelter as solved geometry, the counterculture\'s favorite building.',
    desc: 'Buckminster Fuller\'s geodesic dome — a sphere built of triangulated struts, stronger and lighter as it grows — became the most influential architectural idea of postwar futurism: the 1954 Fuller dome at Milan Triennale, radar domes, Expo 67\'s US Pavilion, and counterculture builders like Drop City (Colorado, 1965) who zome-built domes from car tops and scrap. Fuller\'s rhetoric ("spaceship earth", synergetic geometry) made the dome an icon of rational optimism; off-grid builders, burning-man camps, and the Eden Project keep the geometry alive. The aesthetic: pure triangulated structure, visible engineering, futures drawn in great circles.',
